{{also|go on}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|uːn}}

===Etymology 1===
Shortened from {{term|gooney}}, from obsolete ''gony'' ("simpleton", circa 1580), of unknown origin. ''Gony'' was applied by sailors to the [[albatross]] and similar big, clumsy birds (circa 1839). '''Goon''' first carried the meaning "stupid person" (circa 1921).
* The meaning of "hired thug" (circa 1938) is largely influenced by the comic strip character [[w:Alice the Goon|Alice the Goon]] from the ''[[w:Popeye|Popeye]]'' series.
* The "fool" sense was reinforced by the popular radio program, ''[[w:The Goon Show|The Goon Show]]'', starring [[w:Spike Milligan|Spike Milligan]] and [[w:Peter Sellers|Peter Sellers]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[thug]]; a usually muscular [[henchman]] with little intelligence (also known as a 'hired goon').
# A [[fool]]; someone considered [[silly]], [[stupid]], [[awkward]], or [[outlandish]].
#* {{quote-book|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=5|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=Mr. Campion appeared suitably impressed and she warmed to him. He was very easy to talk to with those long clown lines in his pale face, a natural '''goon''', born rather too early she suspected.}}
# {{context|ice hockey|pejorative|lang=en}}&nbsp; An [[enforcer]] or [[fighter]].

